issue_context.body:
Consider the expression `a+b`.  The regex optimizer recognizes this as the equivalent of:
```
                // ○ Match 'a' atomically at least once.
                // ○ Match 'b'.
```
but the expression `aaa+b` is recognized as the equivalent of:
```
                // ○ Match the string "aa".
                // ○ Match 'a' atomically at least once.
                // ○ Match 'b'.
```
It'd be nice if instead this were recognized as `a{3,}b`.  However, the bigger issue comes when the pattern is slightly rearranged, to instead be `a+aab`.  With that, we get:
```
                // ○ Match 'a' greedily at least once.
                // ○ Advance the next matching position.
                // ○ Match the string "aab".
```
The problem here is the "atomically" vs "greedily".  Because the optimizer combined the "aab" into a _multi_ and only then tried the auto-atomicity, we end up with it not being able to make `a+` atomic because it sees it's followed by an `a`.  And the difference between an atomic loop and a greedy loop is significant when it comes to backtracking and code generation.

This is something a developer can avoid by just writing `a{3,}b` in the first place, as that will produce the desired:
```
                // ○ Match 'a' atomically at least 3 times.
                // ○ Match 'b'.
```
but it'd be nice if the system didn't fight with itself in this manner.

review_comments:
A loop gets emitted as iterating one char at a time, along with extra tracking boilerplate, whereas a string gets emitted as an unrolled/vectorized comparison of the string.  Consider the pattern `aaaaa{10,}\w`.  That gets emitted today as:
```C#
                    // Match the string "aaaa".
                    {
                        byteSpan = global::System.Runtime.InteropServices.MemoryMarshal.AsBytes(slice);
                        if ((uint)slice.Length < 4 ||
                            global::System.Buffers.Binary.BinaryPrimitives.ReadUInt64LittleEndian(byteSpan) != 0x61006100610061ul)
                        {
                            goto NoMatch;
                        }
                    }
                    
                    // Match 'a' greedily at least 10 times.
                    //{
                        pos += 4;
                        slice = slice.Slice(4);
                        charloop_starting_pos = pos;
                        
                        int iteration = 0;
                        while ((uint)iteration < (uint)slice.Length && slice[iteration] == 'a')
                        {
                            iteration++;
                        }
                        
                        if (iteration < 10)
                        {
                            goto NoMatch;
                        }
                        
                        slice = slice.Slice(iteration);
                        pos += iteration;
                        
                        charloop_ending_pos = pos;
                        charloop_starting_pos += 10;
                        goto CharLoopEnd;
                        
                        CharLoopBacktrack:
                        StackPop2(base.runstack, ref stackpos, out charloop_ending_pos, out charloop_starting_pos);
                        
                        if (charloop_starting_pos >= charloop_ending_pos)
                        {
                            goto NoMatch;
                        }
                        pos = --charloop_ending_pos;
                        slice = inputSpan.Slice(pos, end - pos);
                        
                        CharLoopEnd:
                        StackPush2(ref base.runstack!, ref stackpos, charloop_starting_pos, charloop_ending_pos);
                    //}
                    
                    // Match a word character.
                    {
                        if (slice.IsEmpty || !IsWordChar(slice[0]))
                        {
                            goto CharLoopBacktrack;
                        }
                    }
```
The comparison of the first four `a`s happens in a single read/comparison.  If the string were instead coalesced into the loop and it were emitted as `a{14,}\w`, each of those four characters would then be iterated once by one rather than compared in bulk.  So we'd essentially be paying more per character to compare them as part of the repeater rather than as part of the string.  It doesn't seem to be a very measurable difference, which is also why I didn't bother to do the opposite either and try to separate out the min iterations into a multi that comes before it.  These situations also aren't very common.  I think it's worth it for the case I added of a loop followed by a multi, as the impact of removing the backtracking can be huge, but they don't show up very often (there's only 26 patterns in the whole corpus that contain a Oneloop with a lower bound > 1 and an upper bound greater than the lower bound), so the difference in the comparison cost isn't all that much of a motivation.
